WEBVTT

00:05.808 --> 00:15.891
[SPEAKER_00]: You know there's something about human psyche that when we hear the term new technologies, it just excites us. I mean there's a range of emotions

00:16.131 --> 00:25.771
[SPEAKER_00]: that one goes through. It sounds very thrilling to begin with. There's a hope that there's more safety in the newer technology that is coming in.

00:26.852 --> 00:32.153
[SPEAKER_00]: And then you start thinking that there has been a lot of R&D that has gone on behind the scenes. There have been prototypes which have been

00:32.193 --> 00:41.077
[SPEAKER_00]: developed. A lot of test flying has been done by the brand or whosoever is coming out with that new technology in their own field. And there's a

00:41.117 --> 00:50.005
[SPEAKER_00]: very famous kind of saying which goes around in paragliding world that to be the best in paragliding, you have to fly the best equipment. That is

00:50.025 --> 00:58.828
[SPEAKER_00]: the very first thing that you accomplish and then everything else follows it. So this month's topic is all about exploring new

00:58.848 --> 01:07.032
[SPEAKER_00]: technologies in our sport of paragliding. You know, every now and then we see some designer, someone having the will and the zeal and the fire

01:07.172 --> 01:14.635
[SPEAKER_00]: inside them, challenging the status quo and saying that, hey, we're going to do things differently and we're going to add more fun to flying.

01:15.876 --> 01:24.660
[SPEAKER_00]: So I thought, like, who better than bringing in a person who has basically revolutionized flying So far, we are used to seeing

01:24.720 --> 01:32.304
[SPEAKER_00]: paragliders move in a horizontal direction when they're floating through air, and the vertical energy conversion is very limited. But

01:32.384 --> 01:42.288
[SPEAKER_00]: this brand and the people behind it thought outside the box and have created a toy which allows us to expand our horizons of flying

01:42.328 --> 01:52.691
[SPEAKER_00]: paragliders even more. So merging the kiting and the paragliding concept has added a lot of fun in soaring scenarios for now. However, the

01:52.751 --> 02:00.481
[SPEAKER_00]: beauty of this tech is that it is just starting out. And who knows, maybe we are seeing something similar in thermic scenarios. Maybe we are

02:00.501 --> 02:09.431
[SPEAKER_00]: going to see something coming out like that in racing paragliders, in World Cups, and all of that. But We'll leave all of that to the designers,

02:09.912 --> 02:18.281
[SPEAKER_00]: and I hope some of them, the ones which are coming on board this month, will be able to throw some light on what's coming up. But to begin with,

02:18.722 --> 02:27.369
[SPEAKER_00]: this is episode number one of the topic new technologies, and here is a very special man. very passionate person about his craft, Benny

02:27.389 --> 02:35.693
[SPEAKER_00]: Collin. He's the owner of Speed Flying School, has an amazing way of teaching and imparting knowledge, and has been instrumental in

02:35.753 --> 02:43.977
[SPEAKER_00]: bringing this technology to life and making it accessible to everyday pilots like me and you. So without further ado, let's dive right into

02:44.038 --> 02:50.782
[SPEAKER_00]: this very holistic conversation with Benny. Super thankful that he finally came on board and took out time from his busy schedule because

02:50.802 --> 02:58.708
[SPEAKER_00]: I've been chasing him for quite a while. But regardless, it was worth the wait. The content is absolutely amazing and I hope you like it.

03:06.013 --> 03:13.221
[SPEAKER_00]: Benny Galen, so nice to see you finally, man. Welcome to Paralympic Atlas. Thank you for doing this. Thanks for inviting me. Yeah, no, I

03:13.241 --> 03:21.025
[SPEAKER_00]: mean, you know, your content and every single thing that you put out on online is pretty inspiring. And it had been at the back of my mind as a host

03:21.065 --> 03:30.809
[SPEAKER_00]: of a show because this initiative was all about information sharing. I think the way you guys have new technologies coming out in the sport and

03:30.949 --> 03:38.873
[SPEAKER_00]: the amount of fun that is being had with those new technologies, people want to know what goes on behind the scene and how do you guys manage it. So

03:39.173 --> 03:48.476
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, if I have to begin, you know, on some note, speed flying school, like how did this happen in your life? And why did you start? Like, what's

03:48.496 --> 03:57.359
[SPEAKER_00]: the motivation behind such an amazing institute? I basically got into speed flying when I was 20 years old. And I became a paragliding

03:57.399 --> 04:08.902
[SPEAKER_01]: instructor when I was 22. And then I worked for a big school here for many years. And then we separated and I founded my own school. And, you know,

04:08.922 --> 04:16.745
[SPEAKER_01]: there's just in the state where I live here, Bern, there's like 18 paragliding schools. So there's literally at least one school in every

04:16.785 --> 04:25.567
[SPEAKER_01]: valley. So you kind of, I felt like, hey, I want to do something different. So that's kind of why I named my paragliding school Speed

04:25.587 --> 04:34.448
[SPEAKER_01]: Flying School, because we do also teach paragliding, not just speed flying and speed riding. And we also teach paragliding a bit in a

04:34.488 --> 04:43.952
[SPEAKER_01]: different way. We do three weeks courses. We teach people in three weeks in a row in so-called intense courses instead of where people just come

04:43.992 --> 04:52.035
[SPEAKER_01]: and join whenever they want. And then usually they spread it out over a year. So, yeah, we try to do things, you know, a little bit different. And

04:52.095 --> 05:02.460
[SPEAKER_01]: that's why I found like, okay, this name Speed Flying School, it suits also for my paragliding school. No, for sure. And I think your idea of

05:02.540 --> 05:10.205
[SPEAKER_00]: doing things differently seem to be working so well, because I'm pretty sure the amount of passion you have behind the project is turning out

05:10.225 --> 05:17.630
[SPEAKER_00]: some really safe and knowledgeable pilots. But do you want to throw some light on doing things differently, like especially teaching three

05:17.670 --> 05:25.132
[SPEAKER_00]: weeks intensive rather than just, you know, going the other way around like how everybody else plays? Yeah, it's quite interesting. I think

05:25.833 --> 05:33.755
[SPEAKER_01]: globally seen, the standard way to learn paragliding is doing, you know, like a week course, a 10-day course, or like a two-week course. But

05:33.815 --> 05:39.137
[SPEAKER_01]: somehow here in Switzerland, that's really not the standard. The standard here in Switzerland is doing like four or five days, like a

05:39.237 --> 05:48.979
[SPEAKER_01]: basic course with the equipment included. And then... People sign up for the license course, which they have usually like a year time. So a

05:49.059 --> 05:58.126
[SPEAKER_01]: year to get their 50 flights that they need at least to go for the exam here in Switzerland. And then it really spreads out. And there's quite a lot

05:58.146 --> 06:06.393
[SPEAKER_01]: of people along the way that stop because they don't fly in a regular base. And if you don't fly in a regular base, then you never really get the

06:06.433 --> 06:15.319
[SPEAKER_01]: confidence you should, you know. And that subtle feeling we need in that sport, you don't really get that. And yeah, obviously when you do it

06:15.359 --> 06:25.907
[SPEAKER_01]: three weeks in a row and you really fully in there, you commit to it and you get a lot better, a lot more confident and that subtle feeling, that

06:25.947 --> 06:32.532
[SPEAKER_01]: judgment for landings and things. We all know, you know, how it is when you fly in a regular base, when you go on a two weeks paragliding holiday.

06:32.612 --> 06:39.037
[SPEAKER_00]: It just feels better under the canopy. You're just more confident. Exactly. Yeah. Yeah, exactly. You're much more connected to the wing

06:39.097 --> 06:47.385
[SPEAKER_01]: when you do it on a daily base than when you're not flying for a while and you put yourself back on the wing. And that's really, I think, the

06:47.405 --> 06:56.694
[SPEAKER_01]: biggest advantage here for the students. And for me as an instructor, the big advantage is that I have the same people every day. I do get to know

06:56.714 --> 07:07.021
[SPEAKER_01]: them really well. and they all advance as a group. Because normally in the system here we have in Switzerland, it's a very mixed level. The

07:07.041 --> 07:16.568
[SPEAKER_01]: students have between three flights and a hundred flights, you know, and then you as an instructor, you try to teach them all on the same day in

07:16.608 --> 07:23.592
[SPEAKER_01]: the same location you know and some students they they want a thermal and stay up two hours and the other ones would not like to fly at all while

07:23.632 --> 07:34.781
[SPEAKER_01]: there's thermic air and turbulence out there and it's just difficult to you know than to make everyone happy. And here, at least the people have

07:34.881 --> 07:40.447
[SPEAKER_01]: all more or less the same experience. There's still people that want to fly more thermals and some that want to fly less thermals, but at least

07:41.068 --> 07:50.734
[SPEAKER_01]: they have very similar experiences. And that makes it much more easy for me to teach them and to kind of you know, bring in like a continuation, a

07:51.534 --> 07:59.837
[SPEAKER_01]: progress in during the course, you know, start with easy flights with, you know, big landings, easy takeoffs, and then you can progress during

07:59.857 --> 08:06.839
[SPEAKER_01]: the course with the whole group, because otherwise there's always some beginners in the group where you can't take to an advanced landing site

08:06.899 --> 08:14.656
[SPEAKER_01]: or takeoff site because it's early for them. They're not ready for it yet. And here this is a lot less of a problem. makes total sense. And I

08:14.676 --> 08:22.547
[SPEAKER_00]: think safe progression is the word that we should be mentioning here because there are many other ways to progress as well. But if you're

08:22.567 --> 08:31.897
[SPEAKER_00]: shedding your pounds when progressing, then it's not a lot of fun. So progressing safely and confidently But you seem to be pushing the

08:31.957 --> 08:43.724
[SPEAKER_00]: limits quite a lot with your flying yourself as a person. Do you get students who kind of are inspired by your lifestyle and kind of want to

08:43.764 --> 08:52.694
[SPEAKER_00]: follow down your leads and And what I'm trying to understand is the psychology of an instructor. In those cases, do you try to hold back or do

08:52.734 --> 09:01.504
[SPEAKER_00]: you try to give as much as you can, even though it's very early stages for them? Because I certainly resonate when I was kind of learning the

09:01.524 --> 09:10.868
[SPEAKER_00]: sport. I wanted to go all guns blazing. And I did not have enough trust in my first instructor, whether he was giving me the right amount of push

09:10.928 --> 09:20.765
[SPEAKER_00]: and pull at the same time. So do you find those situations, if any, like challenging to navigate? Or what's your way out of those moments? It's

09:20.805 --> 09:34.070
[SPEAKER_01]: funny. I don't think I'm pushing the sport really. I'm just interested in new things. With new technology like a flare system now with those

09:34.130 --> 09:41.833
[SPEAKER_01]: parakites, there's new possibilities popping up. I just like exploring what is possible with these new things. I don't think I do very

09:41.873 --> 09:51.259
[SPEAKER_01]: extreme things. I also fly them quite a lot. They might look extreme for you or for somebody watching it, but Understanding the technology and

09:51.359 --> 10:00.907
[SPEAKER_01]: understanding my experience with that new technology behind it. For me, this is mostly totally comfort zone. Like I try not to do things where

10:00.927 --> 10:08.254
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm scared of. But of course, for other people, this looks like this is crazy and they can't imagine this because they don't know the

10:08.274 --> 10:15.698
[SPEAKER_01]: technology and they don't know what is possible with it and how much experience someone has put into it. That's why it might look crazy. To

10:15.738 --> 10:24.243
[SPEAKER_01]: me, you know, I try to always be in the comfort zone. And that's also where I teach my students like paragliding or also speed flying or speed

10:24.263 --> 10:32.409
[SPEAKER_01]: riding. It should all be done in the comfort zone. You're up in the air and there's always that risk of falling down, you know, like it can be bigger

10:32.449 --> 10:43.663
[SPEAKER_01]: or smaller, but it is gravity and you just can't. cheated whatever goes up can go down and you should never forget about this and lose that

10:43.703 --> 10:53.932
[SPEAKER_01]: respect i think that that's very important and i think sometimes for for people coming from different sports you know like climbing or

10:53.972 --> 11:03.040
[SPEAKER_01]: bodybuilding whatever there are sports that you have to push the limits you know if you're a sports climber and you want to climb uh a or nine grade

11:03.593 --> 11:10.315
[SPEAKER_01]: One day you have to push the limits, you know, you have to keep on going, even if you're scared, even if your hands can't hold it anymore, you

11:10.335 --> 11:19.679
[SPEAKER_01]: still have to push and just go for it until you just slip off and fall into that rope. And if you can't push there, then I think you have a very slow

11:19.719 --> 11:29.644
[SPEAKER_01]: progression. And I think it's super important to understand in those air sports that this is not the case. This might be beneficial when you

11:29.664 --> 11:37.211
[SPEAKER_01]: try to learn awkward maneuvers for a while that when you're, you know, a bit of a bold pilot, you progress a little quicker, but essentially you

11:37.231 --> 11:45.798
[SPEAKER_01]: will get a bad experience and it's going to throw you back a lot and you'll be way more scared than ever before and maybe never even overcome that

11:45.938 --> 11:53.685
[SPEAKER_01]: bad experience anymore, that fear, and you've actually slowed yourself down a lot more than you've gained with pushing a little bit too

11:53.725 --> 12:02.993
[SPEAKER_01]: hard, you know. And I really try to get that to students across. It's sometimes hard, you know, because they see all the things I'm doing and

12:03.734 --> 12:13.059
[SPEAKER_01]: then I tell them not, you know, to push too hard at the same time. But yeah, that's obviously, I'm trying this, yeah. No, I get your dilemma, Vinny.

12:13.379 --> 12:22.145
[SPEAKER_00]: But honestly, you know, the question pops in my mind that you started when you were 20. 20 by no means is an early start in a lot of conventional

12:22.185 --> 12:27.248
[SPEAKER_00]: sports, you know, because I do come from a conventional sports background. And over there, they said that you have to get into it at

12:27.328 --> 12:34.473
[SPEAKER_00]: seven or nine or like, you know, early 10. Like, for example, a soccer player, if you want to be the best in the world. Like by the time you are 14,

12:34.553 --> 12:43.737
[SPEAKER_00]: 15, you're already building up on your international career and some even debut at those like 16, 17 ages. Aerial sports is very different,

12:44.037 --> 12:51.965
[SPEAKER_00]: but it is the glory that the competing or the satisfaction that humans are chasing, that they're looking from competing, is kind of same as it

12:52.045 --> 13:03.692
[SPEAKER_00]: is in conventional sports. You're becoming better of other people and then there's a reward for being better. How did you choose? I mean, even

13:03.732 --> 13:10.875
[SPEAKER_00]: if you didn't push the limits outside, but what was your definition of pushing the limits within comfort zone? And how did you navigate that

13:11.335 --> 13:18.498
[SPEAKER_00]: risk versus reward spectrum while you were trying to get better version of yourself every day? Even though the age that you started was a little

13:18.518 --> 13:26.081
[SPEAKER_00]: bit later, a lot of pilots these days are starting. And I mean, you see pilots flying competition wings at the age of 18, 19 and World Cups

13:26.121 --> 13:35.678
[SPEAKER_00]: already. Well, first of all, I don't think I'm that good of a pilot as you might think. I think there's so many better paragliding pilots out

13:35.718 --> 13:43.620
[SPEAKER_01]: there than I am. I've never done a single paragliding competition. I meant speed flying as well, the whole ecosystem. Many speed fly pilots

13:43.700 --> 13:51.262
[SPEAKER_01]: that can do what I can do or have even a better level and speed riding anyway because I'm not that good of a skier. I really don't think I'm an

13:51.782 --> 14:01.306
[SPEAKER_01]: exceptional pilot. I think some of the things I'm doing is actually pretty easy to achieve for somebody. But

14:01.627 --> 14:11.641
[SPEAKER_01]: sometimes people think I might be such a good pilot is just because I'm kind of exploring that new technology and showing things that haven't

14:11.661 --> 14:19.865
[SPEAKER_01]: been done before. with that new technology but i don't think it's actually that difficult you know and and you know i think with that new

14:19.885 --> 14:28.868
[SPEAKER_01]: technology there's going to be many people that can do things like that you know yeah so so again it's for me it's it's not as extreme i think a lot

14:28.908 --> 14:40.910
[SPEAKER_01]: of it is that new technology that makes things very easy but you know if you don't realize that you think wow this is crazy yeah Now I get it.

14:40.990 --> 14:48.293
[SPEAKER_00]: Instagram versus reality is a different version. And of course, I see you from a six-inch screen every day. So it's a very different

14:48.573 --> 14:57.676
[SPEAKER_00]: perception, I believe. Talking about new technologies, Benny, GoFlare Global basically came in and kind of redefined what having fun

14:57.736 --> 15:08.119
[SPEAKER_00]: means and increased the spectrum. What was the thought process behind? How did this project originate? Because I saw... Jean-Baptiste, you

15:08.139 --> 15:16.462
[SPEAKER_00]: know, putting across a video and like pretty much going crazy. And then like, when I look at the website on like Go Fred Roble, you have, you have

15:16.502 --> 15:25.024
[SPEAKER_00]: the other Benny, Benny Bully and Arwen, like he is one of the legendary, I think he's got an accomplishment about a pilot. So how did, if you didn't

15:25.044 --> 15:31.375
[SPEAKER_00]: just talk about behind the scenes, like how did, how did you guys plan to, to come up with something like this? I wasn't involved from the very

15:31.415 --> 15:41.001
[SPEAKER_01]: beginning. Skywalk has been working on this mustache wing way before I joined them. And they had a pretty much finished product. They just

15:41.061 --> 15:53.975
[SPEAKER_01]: never really dared to sell it or they weren't like, They were undecisive what to market it for. It was a closed cell wing, like the kites. And yeah,

15:54.035 --> 16:02.581
[SPEAKER_01]: somehow they never threw this on the market. And I started flying a lot with the Ozone race kites on Dundee Pila. And I remember exactly my first

16:02.661 --> 16:12.488
[SPEAKER_01]: flight with one of those kites. And I realized, wow, this is awesome. This is a completely different way of flying. And it completely blew me

16:12.548 --> 16:22.481
