[SPEAKER_00]: think it sounds like unlimited fun, basically, whenever you have these wings above your head with the amount of possibilities that you said,

22:47.031 --> 22:56.934
[SPEAKER_00]: you know, within their spectrum. But I'm curious, Benny, like when you explain it that well, what is the most boring flying that Mustache or

22:56.974 --> 23:04.194
[SPEAKER_00]: Lion can offer? Like, where do you start? Like, if you're a pilot or not a pilot, and you don't know what speed wings are, and you don't know what

23:04.314 --> 23:12.898
[SPEAKER_00]: mini wings are, but somehow you see the content and the technology and all of these amazing things out there, and you're keen to get your hands

23:12.998 --> 23:23.283
[SPEAKER_00]: on one of the moustaches and lines. First of all, does it make sense to grab even the bigger sizes of one of these? And secondly, what do baby

23:23.323 --> 23:32.936
[SPEAKER_00]: steps under these wings look like for someone who's coming off from just paragliding background and not smaller wings? Yeah, I think it's super

23:32.976 --> 23:42.299
[SPEAKER_01]: important to realize that a line is made for speed flying and a moustache is made for soaring. So if you're a paraglider pilot and you take a

23:42.339 --> 23:52.002
[SPEAKER_01]: moustache on a normal paragliding takeoff with no soaring possibilities, you can totally take it off there even if there's no

23:52.022 --> 24:00.884
[SPEAKER_01]: wind, you can land it if there's a big landing. It's fairly easy, I think, if you know how to inflate it, how to launch it. People get along pretty

24:00.924 --> 24:14.655
[SPEAKER_01]: quickly, but you can't really get the sense of what this actually can do. So if you really want to try a mustache, I think you almost need to saw it to

24:14.695 --> 24:26.262
[SPEAKER_01]: realize what this thing is capable of doing. I usually tell people, The moment you realize what the mustache is actually capable of is when you

24:26.302 --> 24:35.469
[SPEAKER_01]: stand on a dune and there's like 50 Ks of wind blowing in your face and you stand there with a 15 or 18 meter wing without leaning forward. You just

24:35.509 --> 24:44.533
[SPEAKER_01]: stand there and you think, could be more wind, you know? And you exactly, there's almost sand blowing into your eyes and you know, this is fucking

24:44.573 --> 24:53.176
[SPEAKER_01]: stormy. And you know exactly like, I would not stand here with any other wing, not even with a mini wing, but it's totally easy for Mustache. I

24:53.216 --> 25:01.219
[SPEAKER_01]: think that that's like the moment where you realize what this wing actually, how much this is a game changer, you know? So yeah, basically

25:01.239 --> 25:11.026
[SPEAKER_01]: it makes sense to take a soaring to really really understand the possibility of these things. But it's super easy just to take a 22-meter

25:11.467 --> 25:20.575
[SPEAKER_01]: moustache or even an 18 to a normal takeoff with a little bit of a headwind and you have a bit of a valley breeze at the landing. I think it's really

25:20.615 --> 25:29.000
[SPEAKER_01]: not a big deal. You know, there's a lot of pilots that can do this, you know, and they'll figure out how to fly these things fairly quickly, you

25:29.040 --> 25:36.965
[SPEAKER_01]: know, I would say. I think the most important thing is that you try not to land on a small landing. Same thing with a line. They're kind of hard to,

25:37.245 --> 25:44.070
[SPEAKER_01]: let's say, to make a spot landing at the beginning. You know, usually at the beginning, people end up a little bit, come a little bit short or go a

25:44.090 --> 25:53.289
[SPEAKER_01]: little bit far. Yeah. You kind of have to get used to that a little bit. It usually flares further than they think, you know, and then people come

25:53.329 --> 26:02.451
[SPEAKER_01]: back and they're like, this thing flares forever. And I'm like, that's why it's called flare. I think the way you guys are going swooping and

26:02.471 --> 26:09.372
[SPEAKER_00]: skydiving seems soon going to be out of fashion because paragliders seem to be having a lot of fun with these technologies and for a lot less

26:09.432 --> 26:19.444
[SPEAKER_00]: money, basically. I think so too, yeah. That's amazing. But you know, I was wondering from Paraguay's perspective, when we try and learn a few

26:19.484 --> 26:26.390
[SPEAKER_00]: things in paragliding after kind of graduating from the school, like after having the basic knowledge of an instructor. Now, I personally

26:26.470 --> 26:35.381
[SPEAKER_00]: felt that it was very difficult for anyone to say that, okay, I'm going to put in this much of money every year. And save it up and spend it on SIVs

26:35.421 --> 26:41.892
[SPEAKER_00]: because, okay, you can pay for someone, instructors, up to a certain point, where after that you have to learn and figure it out yourself

26:41.912 --> 26:50.799
[SPEAKER_00]: because it's not financially sustainable to hire an instructor every time you go out there and push the limits. So I naturally tried to start

26:50.819 --> 26:58.181
[SPEAKER_00]: looking at videos and start looking for information that was online and started reading posts and people's experiences. And eventually I was

26:58.201 --> 27:07.164
[SPEAKER_00]: like, okay, if I find a trustworthy instructor sharing his knowledge on YouTube on how to recover asymmetric or how to recover spiral life or any

27:07.204 --> 27:16.449
[SPEAKER_00]: of these SME maneuvers, I can try and gain something out of it. And I can apply it safely in my own training because I have Being under the

27:16.469 --> 27:23.940
[SPEAKER_00]: training of an instructor who has shown me how to do it correctly once, Can something similar be applied in these new technologies where you

27:23.980 --> 27:33.743
[SPEAKER_00]: have YouTube videos where they're teaching you skills? And even though it's a brand new technology, even though you might not have had a chance

27:33.903 --> 27:40.705
[SPEAKER_00]: for an instructor who is specifically trained to impart training on line or flare, because most of the instructors just know how to teach

27:40.745 --> 27:48.446
[SPEAKER_00]: paragliding. There are not a lot of instructors who know how to teach moustache flying or line flying. So can people look at YouTube videos

27:48.486 --> 27:58.073
[SPEAKER_00]: and try and learn something from it while staying safe? Is it a good idea or should everybody just try and reach out to one of the schools who are

27:58.193 --> 28:07.620
[SPEAKER_00]: teaching this and then only put their trust from there? I think it's never wrong going with an instructor. But as you said, there's not that

28:07.660 --> 28:18.702
[SPEAKER_01]: many instructors out there that already have experience with with para kites that can actually teach people, you know, like more that, you

28:18.742 --> 28:29.305
[SPEAKER_01]: know, you can find on our, for example, flair tutorial videos. Anyway, I think, you know, someone that has a decent amount of experience in

28:29.345 --> 28:36.547
[SPEAKER_01]: paragliding, he's going to figure it out really quickly by himself. Someone that is pretty new that has basically just got his paragliding

28:36.587 --> 28:49.730
[SPEAKER_01]: license. He can go on a moustache or even on a line pretty quickly as well, but he needs to be smart about it. So both wings, they have quite a lot of

28:49.790 --> 29:00.074
[SPEAKER_01]: roll. So I think you have to be able to deal with the roll that you don't start rolling around uncontrolled. And you have to be able to deal with

29:00.114 --> 29:11.081
[SPEAKER_01]: the speed or you have to, you know, be able to learn with it. So you have to fly a big enough size first, you know, and not go on a too small size too

29:11.121 --> 29:19.446
[SPEAKER_01]: quick. I think for both wings, it's great to start with ground handling, obviously. Run around on a field and, you know, get to know the stall

29:19.487 --> 29:27.371
[SPEAKER_01]: point, get to know where the, you know, the maximum lift is and everything. And if you've done that, you're going to be already very

29:27.411 --> 29:38.679
[SPEAKER_01]: confident on launch. And landing is also going to be less of a problem. And Yeah, especially sawing a moustache, I think you can really, if you

29:38.699 --> 29:49.124
[SPEAKER_01]: have a good spot, like sandy spots on dunes, you can really learn that yourself easily, you know. That's why we have made those tutorial

29:49.144 --> 30:00.650
[SPEAKER_01]: videos, you know, on YouTube with Flair, to give people, pilots, the knowledge they need to have to fly these things. But it's funny because

30:01.250 --> 30:09.337
[SPEAKER_01]: the other brands don't do this for their standard paragliders. You get the impression that these things are super extreme. Like a moose is such

30:09.357 --> 30:17.763
[SPEAKER_01]: an extreme wing and you need a special education and everything to be able to fly one. It's not at all like this. It's just because the mustache

30:17.783 --> 30:28.632
[SPEAKER_01]: was the first parakeet out there. And there's some things, you know, there's some bad habits that, you know, that can fuck you up on launch if

30:28.672 --> 30:38.139
[SPEAKER_01]: you're doing this. And that's why we made those videos, you know. But it's very few things. And I think for many pilots, it's pretty

30:38.179 --> 30:47.806
[SPEAKER_01]: straightforward. I'm always surprised how quickly people get along with this. I have put a lot of people on a moustache and already on a line,

30:48.146 --> 31:00.851
[SPEAKER_01]: and I'm always surprised how quickly people figure this out. That's good to hear. It looks something really fun. So you obviously figure out

31:00.891 --> 31:09.615
[SPEAKER_00]: that if it's a lot of fun, that means it's a lot of work to get to that fun part. But if you're saying it's quick, me, including a lot of listeners,

31:09.635 --> 31:17.556
[SPEAKER_00]: will be stoked to give it a shot one day. Yeah, exactly. I think the most important thing is, in general, is try it in forgiving terrain. So if you

31:17.576 --> 31:25.844
[SPEAKER_01]: want to go on a line, if you have skis and you have an easy takeoff, easy terrain, go do it on skis. If you want to try a moustache, go somewhere

31:25.864 --> 31:34.071
[SPEAKER_01]: there is not a cliff where you have to top land for soaring, you know. Go somewhere with a beach, easy launch, you can land on the beach, maybe

31:34.171 --> 31:43.924
[SPEAKER_01]: even sand dunes. And in this environment, you can really progress a lot and even make some mistakes and it's quite forgiving. I think that's

31:43.964 --> 31:52.536
[SPEAKER_01]: kind of the most important thing if you want to go and try, teach this yourself without an instructor, that you have a forgiving landscape

31:52.576 --> 32:02.106
[SPEAKER_01]: around you. Now it makes sense that the safety net should be as vague and as broad as possible before. Exactly. Yeah. Yeah. Then it takes me to a

32:02.166 --> 32:09.704
[SPEAKER_00]: point, you know, if there is someone out there listening, like, you know, I have had that mindset myself some of the times. where I'm like,

32:10.445 --> 32:17.688
[SPEAKER_00]: this seems to be within my comfort zone, and I would like to push the limits a bit more. We all have that, I think, inherent part of our brain

32:17.728 --> 32:24.912
[SPEAKER_00]: telling us that you are capable of doing this, and even though it looks a bit scarier, but you want to. So, I'm just wondering, if there is someone

32:24.952 --> 32:36.837
[SPEAKER_00]: who does not come from coast, who lives in the foothills, and is living in a thermic site, still decides to take this up on a thermic site, what is

32:36.897 --> 32:44.984
[SPEAKER_00]: the worst that That can happen. Like, how does shitting your pants under a moustache look like? Because under a pantomime, it's just like, there

32:45.004 --> 32:51.170
[SPEAKER_00]: are collapses, and there can be cascades, and there can be twists, and then, you know, it's history. Then you deploy the result. But, like,

32:51.430 --> 33:00.358
[SPEAKER_00]: what can happen in a parrachide if you put it in thermic conditions in the mountain? I don't exactly know. I've never had a collapse.

33:04.631 --> 33:13.541
[SPEAKER_01]: But I tell people, just be smart about it. You can totally fly them through thermals. We just marketed the mustache to not fly in thermals.

33:14.560 --> 33:26.904
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it's so easy to fly this wing in strong wind. And strong wind, you know, with 50 Ks of wind, the wind has four times the energy than 25 Ks of

33:26.964 --> 33:35.127
[SPEAKER_01]: wind when you're normally flying. So the turbulence, if you hit a lee side in 50 Ks, is also four times as bad. And then if you have 50 Ks of wind

33:35.227 --> 33:45.758
[SPEAKER_01]: and you have strong thermals in there on top and people fly hands up, also the moustache is going to collapse at some stage. We knew the Moustache

33:45.778 --> 33:54.653
[SPEAKER_01]: is pretty collapse stable. It's more collapse stable than any other paraglider out there flying on full bar. But the thing is, on a

33:54.713 --> 34:06.218
[SPEAKER_01]: paraglider, there is this psychological... hurdle like anyone knows that has a bit of experience if i push that speed bar i'm faster but i'm

34:06.299 --> 34:16.541
[SPEAKER_01]: more likely to get a collapse and if i do get a collapse more likely to lose control at least anyone that has done an siv and pulled some you know bar

34:16.581 --> 34:26.423
[SPEAKER_01]: asymmetrics has that respect built in from then on definitely yeah but then it hits you yeah exactly you feel it and you get that respect you know

34:26.463 --> 34:35.391
[SPEAKER_01]: that's the good thing about doing an siv So there's that psychological barrier of pushing that speed bar. And with a parakite, with those

34:35.451 --> 34:44.583
[SPEAKER_01]: risers, this is completely gone. It's super easy to go hands up. And people go hands up all the time. There's zero psychological barrier of

34:44.763 --> 34:54.826
[SPEAKER_01]: going full speed. And we were aware of this. And that's why we tried to keep people safe and warn people like, hey, this thing is super stable,

34:54.926 --> 35:05.011
[SPEAKER_01]: even full bar, which is only hands up. You don't have to push anything. But it can still collapse. And that's why we've marketed like don't fly

35:05.051 --> 35:11.414
[SPEAKER_01]: in thermals and blah, blah, blah. But in fact, it works perfectly. as well as on a paraglider, or actually even better because it's more

35:11.454 --> 35:21.639
[SPEAKER_01]: stable. But we didn't want for people to fly brainless in 50, 60 k's of wind with strong thermic in there because we knew they were going to go

35:21.699 --> 35:28.902
[SPEAKER_01]: hands up without thinking about it. And that's the whole reason behind it. So when people want to fly through thermals, I just tell them, go for

35:28.962 --> 35:38.847
[SPEAKER_01]: it, do it, but fly in best glide, which is quite low on your hands. That's the normal trim position on a paraglider. Don't fly hands up. And then

35:38.907 --> 35:48.609
[SPEAKER_01]: your wing is maybe not more stable. Probably it is more stable, but if you do get a collapse, you're not flying fully accelerated. So if you do get a

35:48.669 --> 35:59.272
[SPEAKER_01]: collapse when flying slow, the collapse is going to be a lot more gentle and you can react to it better, I guess. So basically that's what I

35:59.312 --> 36:08.454
[SPEAKER_01]: recommend people. Just fly through thermal slowly and fly active and then there's no worries about it. What's the behaviour difference

36:08.514 --> 36:16.494
[SPEAKER_00]: between a moustache and a regular paraglider? How would an SIV look under a moustache compared to a regular paraglider? That's a good

36:16.514 --> 36:25.917
[SPEAKER_01]: question. You can't pull collapses on a moustache. You can do pull-ups on the A-lines. Even with the collapse lines? Yeah, if you have a pulling

36:25.957 --> 36:33.419
[SPEAKER_01]: line, it would work, yes. It is actually pretty gentle, the reaction from the moustache to collapses, because the wing is quite soft. It's

36:33.499 --> 36:43.023
[SPEAKER_01]: not tensioned a lot. It's built in a very forgiving way. Surprisingly forgiving. I would say it's not any worse than a sea glider. It's just a

36:43.063 --> 36:55.051
[SPEAKER_01]: rough guess. But when you take a collapse and you go hands up, then the glider is accelerated. And a glider that is fully accelerated, it... It

36:55.071 --> 37:03.917
[SPEAKER_01]: doesn't have a lot of pressure inside, so it takes a long time to inflate, to reinflate. You can very well notice this when you do big ears and you

37:03.957 --> 37:13.223
[SPEAKER_01]: have the speed bar pushed and then you let go the A-line. Then on a lot of gliders, the A's, the ears just stay in. They don't inflate until you

37:13.303 --> 37:20.369
[SPEAKER_01]: release the speed bar. So it's a little bit the same thing with a moustache. If you get a big collapse and you're just staying hands up, it

37:20.409 --> 37:31.258
[SPEAKER_01]: takes a very, very long, quite a long time to fully inflate the gun all the way into the tips. It works much quicker if you actually countersteer

37:31.398 --> 37:38.684
[SPEAKER_01]: and pump it open. If you give it a pump, the collapse side, then it re-inflates very quickly. I think that's like the main difference, you

37:38.724 --> 37:49.754
[SPEAKER_01]: know. A fair comparison would be taking a normal paraglider to an SIV and make a full speed bar collapse and stay on speed bar and stay hands up.

37:50.734 --> 38:05.485
[SPEAKER_00]: Wow. Man, that's a recipe for disaster. Yeah, exactly. That's scary. And on a Mustang, this is even surprisingly... gentle like so wow this is

38:05.525 --> 38:15.338
[SPEAKER_01]: just a guess but i think the mustache it it would actually be more gentle than than some other sea wings in in that case you know but we don't have a

38:15.378 --> 38:24.404
[SPEAKER_01]: lot of experiences with collapses because they don't happen much nothing That's such a good way to put it. But I'm just wondering, when

38:24.424 --> 38:32.930
[SPEAKER_00]: you're developing a wing like this, of course, there must have been some history that would have gone into the project before it was restarted

38:32.970 --> 38:42.996
[SPEAKER_00]: again. But what effort does the manufacturer or the production team put when it comes to trying and feeling what life under it looks like when it

38:43.076 --> 38:51.122
[SPEAKER_00]: is not so good? Do you guys take it out and... just keep on collapsing it again and again and keep on... Like, how does testing look like? What did

38:51.142 --> 38:57.888
[SPEAKER_00]: your protos kind of do when you were... Like, what were the possibilities at that time, if you can just talk about that? That's an

38:57.908 --> 39:07.106
[SPEAKER_01]: interesting question. In fact, I think the paragliding brands, they do this a lot because they have to pass that certification. So... For the

39:07.166 --> 39:15.268
[SPEAKER_01]: normal paraglider test pilots, it's very important that their wing has this exact, how do you say, behavior. Recovery behavior, I think.

39:15.308 --> 39:23.530
[SPEAKER_01]: Recovery behavior that it can pass that certification. And they put a lot of effort to reach this. We didn't do that effort. We put a lot of

39:23.670 --> 39:33.169
[SPEAKER_01]: effort into, or especially Benny, he's the genius behind it. I'm just telling him, no, it's got to go faster or whatever. got to do this better,

39:33.189 --> 39:42.832
[SPEAKER_01]: whatever. But he's the genius behind it. I don't understand 10% of what he does and understands, you know. But basically, we just focus on

39:43.052 --> 39:54.376
[SPEAKER_01]: making these things stable enough that collapses do not happen in those conditions it's made for. I think when you're soaring or when you're

39:54.396 --> 40:03.102
[SPEAKER_01]: speed flying close to the ground, it doesn't matter too much if you get a full speed bar collapse and your wing turns 130 degrees or 150 degrees.

40:03.122 --> 40:13.890
[SPEAKER_01]: You're going to hit the ground anyway. You know what I mean? It's much more important that your wing does not collapse while you're flying

40:13.930 --> 40:24.256
[SPEAKER_01]: close to the ground. So... That, I think, is a much more important case also when it comes to speed flying wings, you know. And I think it's the

40:24.316 --> 40:32.644
[SPEAKER_01]: same with, say, paramotor wings, you know. Paramotor wings are also the full reflex glide. Yeah, yeah, yeah. They just keep on going. Yeah.

40:33.422 --> 40:40.870
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly. For those, it's important not to have a gentle collapse behavior. It's important to not collapse at all. That's kind of a very

40:40.910 --> 40:49.058
[SPEAKER_01]: different mentality in a way. In paragliding, everyone is worried about how it does collapse, but I actually rather have a wing that does

40:49.078 --> 41:00.913
[SPEAKER_01]: not collapse at all. first hand and you know the second my second worry is how the reaction is going to be but I'm actually happier flying a wing

41:00.953 --> 41:11.495
[SPEAKER_01]: that does not collapse you know what I mean it's it's a very different mentality in a way I find it very easy to resonate with it because when I

41:11.535 --> 41:16.859
[SPEAKER_00]: talk to non-flyers, you know, when I talk to my friends who don't fly, they're like, dude, that's stupid. Like, why are you doing this? You

41:16.879 --> 41:23.183
[SPEAKER_00]: know, why are you going out in the air? And the answer that you gave me in the beginning when I was stoked that, you know, the amount of effort and

41:23.204 --> 41:30.769
[SPEAKER_00]: the way you fly is like, it seems very exciting. Like the risk versus reward seems to be a lot more higher. So I get that question from my

41:30.869 --> 41:39.554
[SPEAKER_00]: non-flying friends. And I'm like the, the, the obvious, like, you know, school of thought is that I want to be under a wing or I'm flying an

41:39.574 --> 41:47.698
[SPEAKER_00]: equipment that does not fail. Like I trust in the, the equipment failure should not happen. That is the first rule of the game. That is why I play

41:47.718 --> 41:54.521
[SPEAKER_00]: the game. And I think that is what you're saying that the first rule of this game is that the collapse does not come. And that is why we are

41:54.541 --> 42:03.102
[SPEAKER_00]: playing it because that rule is usually it holds true. So when we're trying to build collapse resistant, you know, when we're like putting

42:03.322 --> 42:10.344
[SPEAKER_00]: most of our eggs in rule number one, in rule number one's basket, you know, because that is like, it has to be collapse resistant, then we can

42:10.384 --> 42:21.588
[SPEAKER_00]: have more fun. Why can't we get inspiration from those kites that have like, is collapse a safety behavior? Like why can't we make wings that

42:21.608 --> 42:29.509
[SPEAKER_00]: just don't collapse? Like what's stopping the manufacturers? I think you have to ask the designers. I'm not a designer. I'm not the genius. But

42:29.829 --> 42:37.994
[SPEAKER_01]: I think it has a little bit to do with it, with that certification for the paragliders. As I said, the certification is about making a gentle

42:38.014 --> 42:49.829
[SPEAKER_01]: collapse behavior, which in a way stops manufacturers maybe sometimes of building a wing that is very collapse resistant. You can make a wing

42:49.869 --> 43:00.296
[SPEAKER_01]: more, more collapse resistant, for example, by having the A-lines for the back. But if you have the A-lines for the back, you cannot, it gets

43:00.336 --> 43:11.185
[SPEAKER_01]: very hard to certify the wings because collapses tend to be bigger and deeper. And then also it doesn't work as good for, for SIVs. Yeah. So, but

43:11.385 --> 43:20.856
[SPEAKER_01]: I think, you know, that technology with those reflex airfoils That is rather new. I mean, there's many paragliders out there that actually

43:20.916 --> 43:29.490
[SPEAKER_01]: have reflex built in and people just don't know. So the mustache is not like this crazy new wing that has a technology that no other wing had.

43:30.208 --> 43:38.630
[SPEAKER_01]: There's quite many gliders out there that have a reflex airfoil in there. The Mustache is just the first wing that has combined this with

43:38.910 --> 43:50.633
[SPEAKER_01]: those kite risers, you know, with those flare system. But my opinion or, you know, my experience is that there's quite a lot of wings that don't

43:50.713 --> 44:04.232
[SPEAKER_01]: have reflex. And I think that reflex technology is still kind of a little bit in the early stages, you know, I think. Brands have been very focused

44:04.332 --> 44:13.898
[SPEAKER_01]: on making paragliders that have a lot of performance. Climb well and that glide really well. Like that you race, basically. Yeah, but for any

44:13.958 --> 44:24.125
[SPEAKER_01]: class, you know, like even B-Wings, you know, have to have a very good performance. But I think in the future that performance can no longer be

44:24.245 --> 44:33.486
[SPEAKER_01]: improved that much. Because you reach a plateau, you know, think there's only so much glide you can get with a wing that has aspect ratio

44:33.526 --> 44:43.951
[SPEAKER_01]: five and 40 cells. So I hope that in the future, the brands will start making wings that are more collapse stable, that are safer and focus

44:44.031 --> 44:53.813
[SPEAKER_01]: less on the performance. I think that's what our market needs and I think there's quite big potential, but This is just a rough guess, you know,

44:53.853 --> 45:04.243
[SPEAKER_01]: like I could be totally wrong. Maybe there won't get much more collapse stable, but I think there is quite some room for improvement for making

45:04.903 --> 45:13.818
[SPEAKER_01]: any of those gliders quite a bit more collapse stable than they are now. But I think it might take time. I have to ask you something very honestly,

45:13.859 --> 45:22.806
[SPEAKER_00]: Benny here, because this question has been ringing in my mind ever since I graduated from the school. Because the first question that a student

45:22.846 --> 45:32.873
[SPEAKER_00]: gets after graduating is, should I buy an A wing or should I buy a low B wing? And when you come out as a layman, when you're just learning to see

45:32.914 --> 45:41.120
